      "instance_name": "travis_scott",
      "label": "Travis Scott (Artist)",
      "description": "The person in the bottom panel is Travis Scott, a well-known American rapper and singer. His image is used here to represent the 'boys reaction' to being sent out of class. His public persona and stage presence are often associated with high-energy, flamboyant performances, which the meme uses to contrast with the 'girls reaction'.",
      "belongsToAnalysis": {
        "@id": "http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#analysis_3167fd09"
      },
      "extractionMethod": "llm_extraction_huggingface"
    },
    {
      "@id": "http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#dimension_e87a76bb",
      "@type": "BackgroundKnowledge",
      "instance_name": "teacher_says_get_out_of_class",
      "label": "School Discipline Scenario: Teacher Saying 'Get Out of Class'",
      "description": "This is a common school-related scenario where a teacher removes a student from class, often for disruptive behavior. The meme uses this as a trigger for contrasting reactions, implying that boys are more likely to react with excitement or rebellion, while girls react with distress. This is a social stereotype about gendered responses to authority.",
      "belongsToAnalysis": {
        "@id": "http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#analysis_3167fd09"
      },
      "extractionMethod": "llm_extraction_huggingface"
    },
    {
      "@id": "http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#dimension_4c68dacf",
      "@type": "BackgroundKnowledge",
      "instance_name": "gendered_reaction_stereotype",
      "label": "Gendered Reaction Stereotype",
      "description": "This stereotype suggests that girls are more emotionally reactive and prone to distress in social or academic situations, while boys are more likely to react with excitement, rebellion, or aggression. The meme uses this to set up the contrast between the two reactions, reinforcing a harmful gender binary.",
      "belongsToAnalysis": {
